\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Block half-turn and three arithmetic points}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{In the coordinate order of \ref{fdip-000W}, retained
coordinates #{(0,2)} and eliminated coordinates #{(1,3)}, the
half-turn restricts to
#{J=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)}
on both blocks. The three rational points
#{t=-2}, #{t=-1} and #{t=1} separate the regularity loci.
At #{t=-2}, #{\det Q=9} and #{R=3/2}, while the eliminated
block vanishes. At #{t=-1}, #{\det Q=-3} and #{\det S=-3}.
At #{t=1}, #{\det Q=45}, #{\det S=5} and #{R=3/8}.}

\proof{
\p{The equal diagonal and equal off-diagonal entries of the
effective matrix #{S} give #{J^TSJ=S}. Retained projection
#{\pi} satisfies #{\pi P=J\pi}, but is not an isometry:
#{q(\delta_1)=s} is a unit on #{R_{\mathrm{elim}}}, whereas
#{\pi\delta_1=0}. Thus
[Clifford functoriality](fdip-000Q) supplies no map induced by
this projection. The effective rank-two Clifford algebra and
the full rank-4 algebra are different constructions.}

\p{Substitute into #{\kappa=3(2t+1)(2t+3)} and into the trace
ratio of \ref{fdip-000W}. At #{t=-2}, #{\kappa=9} and
#{R=3/2}, but #{C_{\mathrm{block}}=s\bbI_2=0}. This point belongs
to #{U_R} inside #{X_q} and lies outside #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}}.
At #{t=-1}, #{\kappa=-3}, #{C_{\mathrm{block}}=\bbI_2}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\begin{pmatrix}-1&2\\2&-1\end{pmatrix},\\
\det S&=-3.
\end{aligned}}
This point belongs to #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}}, but the trace
ratio has a pole, so it lies outside #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}}.
At #{t=1}, #{\kappa=45}, #{C_{\mathrm{block}}=3\bbI_2}, and
##{\begin{aligned}
S&=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}7&-2\\-2&7\end{pmatrix},\\
\det S&=5,\qquad R=\frac38.
\end{aligned}}
This point belongs to #{X_{\mathrm{obs}}}.}
}
